  "narrative": {
    "intro": "FY2026 to date federal contract obligations in Indiana total $3.5B. The Department of Defense obligated $2.4B, making the DoD by far the largest buyer in the state this fiscal year. Award activity includes 16,794 awards with an average award size of $210K. Obligations fell 49.7% year over year, and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date). Fiscal history shows obligations of $6.3B in FY21, $8.2B in FY22, $6B in FY23, $5.3B in FY24, and $7B in FY25. Major non-DoD buyers in FY2026 to date include the Department of Health and Human Services with $285M and the Department of Homeland Security with $263M. Leading vendors include ROLLS-ROYCE CORPORATION at $816M and NATIONAL GOVERNMENT SERVICES, INC. at $266M. Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing leads industries at $736M, followed by Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $334M and Engineering Services at $308M. Treat FY2026 to date totals as partial fiscal-year figures.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal buying in Indiana this year?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $2.4B of the state total. The next two largest agencies are the Department of Health and Human Services with $285M and the Department of Homeland Security with $263M. Top vendors and top industries further concentrate spending, for example ROLLS-ROYCE CORPORATION at $816M and Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing at $736M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How has Indiana’s federal contracting trended recently?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 49.7% year over year in FY2026 to date compared with the prior fiscal period. Annual obligations were $6.3B in FY21, $8.2B in FY22, $6B in FY23, $5.3B in FY24, and $7B in FY25, before the current $3.5B total for FY26 to date. Treat the FY2026 figure as partial because current-year totals are fiscal year to date."
      }
    ]
